UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 2, Lesson 4: Storytelling and oral recount', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled flashback is used, and checks listener understanding with a brief question or pause. Cue cards beat full scripts for natural delivery.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nReduce a busy paragraph to five cue words and write one listener-check question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Cues name turning points; question targets the goal or obstacle, not trivia.\n\nCHECK\nReading every adjective from a page. Oral recount prioritises sequence and salience.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPrepare cue cards for an original four-beat recount, perform it, note one listener response and one timing improvement.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled flashback is used, and checks listener understanding with a brief question or pause. Cue cards beat full scripts for natural delivery.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and listening","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Prepare cue cards for an original four-beat recount, perform it, note one listener response and one timing improvement. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using oral recount on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Reading every adjective from a page.. Correction: Strong answer: Cues name turning points;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Reading every adjective from a page. Oral recount prioritises sequence and salience. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating oral recount on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with oral recount, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about oral recount with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for oral recount. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for oral recount. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Cues name turning points","Reading every adjective from a page","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Reduce a busy paragraph to five cue words and write one listener-check question. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for oral recount; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading every adjective from a page","An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled","Cues name turning points","Careful use of chronology and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with oral recount?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named chronology and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Reading every adjective from a page","Name chronology and explain why it matters only, without linking it to oral recount","Apply oral recount to new material and name the chronology and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies oral recount with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries oral recount. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Reading every adjective from a page","Point to exact words in the sentence that show oral recount and say what they do"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses oral recount from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading every adjective from a page","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name chronology and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about oral recount?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse chronology and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for oral recount?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new","Reading every adjective from a page","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about oral recount","Retype the worked model as the final answer"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of oral recount on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about oral recount with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating oral recount on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using oral recount on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for oral recount. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with oral recount, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Reading every adjective from a page.. Correction: Strong answer: Cues name turning points;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w02-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise oral recount.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use oral recount with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify chronology and explain why it matters.","I can practise salience independently on a new prompt.","I can notice listener check needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled flashback is used, and checks listener understanding with a brief question or pause.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral recount with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally and one listener check: ''What was Mara trying to do before visitors came?''\n\nTRY FIRST\nReduce a busy paragraph to five cue words and write one listener-check question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Cues name turning points; question targets the goal or obstacle, not trivia.","explain":"An oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled flashback is used, and checks listener understanding with a brief question or pause. Cue cards beat full scripts for natural delivery.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reading every adjective from a page. Oral recount prioritises sequence and salience. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Oral recount.","caption":"Select what matters.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Salient</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">turning points</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Skip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">every breath</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Cues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Cues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral recount with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally and one listener check: ''What was Mara trying to do before visitors came?'' Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Delivery tools.","caption":"Natural, not scripted.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five words</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">listener question</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral recount with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReading every adjective from a page. Oral recount prioritises sequence and salience. Here is a more productive model: Cues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally and one listener check: ''What was Mara trying to do before visitors came?''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Cues name turning points; question targets the goal or obstacle, not trivia.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Reading every adjective from a page. Oral recount prioritises sequence and salience. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about oral recount with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn oral storytelling recount selects salient events (not every breath), keeps chronology clear unless a signalled flashback is used, and checks listener understanding with a brief question or pause. Cue cards beat full scripts for natural delivery. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 2, Lesson 4: Storytelling and oral recount","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: short stories and voice","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nCues: hall—blocked door; side light—entry; display—set with two minutes left. Spoken recount uses First / Next / Finally and one listener check: ''What was Mara trying to do before visitors came?''\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Oral recount.","caption":"Select what matters.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Salient</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">turning points</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Skip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">every breath</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Delivery tools.","caption":"Natural, not scripted.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five words</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">listener question</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s4-eng-y7-w02-l4' AND family_id IS NULL;
